/*******************************************************************************
                    incant/setup -- the commentary

    WHY IT IS DOWN HERE. Tony's ruling, 2026-09-16: the definitions above are a
    reference table and they are read as one, so argument wedged between the rows
    gets in the way of the thing the file is for. The same split the comment
    convention makes inside a method -- MOVE WHAT ARGUES, KEEP WHAT DESCRIBES --
    applied to a registry file. The short slug comments beside individual rows
    stay where they are; what moved is the long-form reasoning, one section per
    topic rather than one per line.

    ⚠ THIS IS NOT incant/directives' SHAPE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED.
    directives ends in bail() and carries its guide in a parse-dead region.
    setup cannot: bail() pops a DIVERTED input, and GroupMain loads setup with
    pushInput at a moment when sourceFILE is still null, so pushInput never
    raises inputDiverted and setup sits at the BOTTOM of the input stack with
    nothing to hand back to. A bail() here is inert -- measured 2026-09-16, prose
    placed under one was parsed and answered with three RunRulE complaints. So
    this region is an ordinary comment and is subject to every comment hazard.

    ⚠ ONE OPEN, ONE CLOSE, AND NO NESTED COMMENT MARKER ANYWHERE INSIDE. Bear-trap
    27: a comment carrying indented code-shaped text can kill the parse, silently,
    at exit 138 with no output -- and this file is read at runtime by every single
    run, so a break here takes down the whole fleet rather than one fixture. Keep
    additions to prose. Quote a spelling inline with backticks, never as an
    indented line of its own.


    1. THE INSTALL VOCABULARY -- parseMethod, parseTerms, setParse, parseClassify

    Promoted from incant/genScratch 2026-08-05 by Tony's ruling, and they join the
    isRule family deliberately: these are define-time fire-and-forget commands and
    they must be commands WHERE THE GRAMMAR IS READ, not only inside the fixture
    that tests them.

    ⚠ WHAT IT COST TO LEARN: registered only in genScratch, a `parseMethod=parseBraced`
    written into incant/grammar was not a command, so it was never fired and never
    consumed -- it was parsed as an ORDINARY TERM. Braced went from 3 terms to 5
    against a generated method indexing rule 1 through 3, and oneTest died at
    SIGSEGV. Every Scaf rule is defined inside genScratch itself, so the install
    route had only ever been exercised in the one file that defines the words it
    needs.


    2. THE BOOLEAN OPERATORS -- `||`, `&&`, `shortCircuit`, `isOR`

    THE SYMBOL WAS FILLED 2026-08-01 (Tony's word). Demand was demonstrated by
    minionA round 2, which hit this and wrote twin sequential guards in `spellMany`
    instead of one disjunction.

    ⚠ IT WAS A REGISTRATION, NOT AN IMPLEMENTATION. Boolean OR already existed and
    was already sound -- `opOR` lives in Instruct.rtn and `testOR` exercised it in
    the unitTests baseline. What was missing was only the SYMBOL spelling: `'|'` is
    registered BARE, with no operateMethod, so `||` matched an inert operator and
    failed the body. `'||'` is registered explicitly rather than relying on `'|'`
    doubling, because the Operators header states the matcher returns the LONGEST
    match -- so the two-character entry wins cleanly and `'|'` keeps whatever future
    single-character meaning it is being held for.

    ⚠ `shortCircuit` IS A REGISTRATION, NOT A LIST, AND IT REPLACED TWO LISTS RATHER
    THAN ONE -- the tier-3 SELECTION in interpretXP and the SKIP ITSELF inside
    runShortCircuit, which both asked whether the operator's tag was AND or OR. That
    is why `||` did not short-circuit while the word form did, on the same
    operateMethod: measured 2026-09-10 with a side effect, `true OR loud()` fired the
    right arm 0 times and `true || loud()` fired it once. ⚠ AND FIXING ONLY THE
    SELECTION MADE `true || false` READ FALSE, because the second list then sent `||`
    past both skips into the right-arm evaluation. ONE LIST HID ANOTHER.
    `isOR` names WHICH way it short-circuits; the two together are the whole
    contract, and no spelling appears in either action.

    ⚠ THE WORD FORMS ARE RETIRED, 2026-09-10. `AND` and `OR` were registered here
    until the C spellings became correct; they are gone, and the only boolean
    operators in the language are `&&` and `||`. What made the rename possible was
    not the registration -- `||` carried opOR for weeks -- but the two SPELLING LISTS
    becoming registrations, one choosing the tier-3 binding and one choosing the skip
    direction. Until both fell, `||` evaluated its right operand and the word did not.


    3. THE PREFIX UNARIES -- `deref` and `accessClass`

    `deref` is the unary star that replaces auto-unwrapping. It is NAMED rather than
    symbol-spelled, for exactly the reason `negate` is: handleUnary re-points a prefix
    star here so the BINARY star slot (opMultiply, three consumers) is never touched.
    Same two-slot separation, same reason.

    `accessClass` IS A REGISTRATION RATHER THAN A LIST IN THE ACTION. Ruled
    try-and-buy 2026-09-10: a prefix unary is ACCESS class, binding to the PRIMARY
    before the postfix chain, or VALUE class, binding to the chain's RESULT. A
    starred `A.B` is a starred A, then dotted B; a negated `A.B` negates the whole of
    `A.B`. Only the ACCESS class carries the attribute, so the predicate is presence
    and the default is value.

    ⚠ IT GOES ON THE STAR OPERATOR AND NOT ON `deref`, because the class is asked
    BEFORE handleUnary maps the token to its named operator.


    4. THE RETIRED SET-POINTER OPERATOR -- its obituary

    Retired 2026-08-30, ruled by Tony. It was the manual way to set isPointer -- the
    flag that exempts a field from runOP's auto-unwrap. It could never be USED,
    because the unwrap it exempts from runs BEFORE the operator that sets it: the
    mechanism intercepted its own repair. That is what prompted the unary-star ruling.

    Measured 2026-08-30 across 178 fixtures and 29,634 runOP entries: isPointer
    suppressed an unwrap ZERO times, against 5,447 unwraps fired and 2,815 isIterator
    suppressions -- so the not-isPointer clause in all four unwrap lines is a
    never-true condition with no live customers. `deref` is its successor.

    ⚠ `opPointer` THE EXTERN SURVIVES: it is still reached as the `setPointer`
    definition-time command in the cOMMANDs registry above, used by bcPushField,
    which is a different spelling with a real user.


    5. THE THREE SLOTS -- operateMethod, interpretMethod, jitEmitter

    The star operator is the step-2 pathfinder, the first op to carry a jitEmitter.
    Three slots, three consumers, one op: operateMethod is the interpreter,
    interpretMethod is the bytecode handler, jitEmitter is the JIT. They sit side by
    side deliberately; installing the third disturbs neither of the first two, which
    is what makes the migration one op at a time.


    6. THE MODIFIER CLASS

    Ruled 2026-09-10: A FLAG DESCRIBES A TERM; A REPETITION CHANGES WHAT THE TERM IS.
    Only the REPETITION class carries `repeatClass`, so the predicate is presence and
    the default is flag -- the same shape `accessClass` uses for the unary split, and
    for the same reason: a class you must not forget to declare is a class that will
    be forgotten.

    ⚠ THE CLASS LIVES IN THE Modifiers REGISTRY AND NOWHERE ELSE. modifyClass asks
    one predicate; there is no list of characters in any action. Adding a modifier is
    an edit to that block.

    ⚠ AND THE CURLY BRACES ARE FLAGS DESPITE LOOKING LIKE A LIMIT. They raise upTo
    and unGuarded -- facts ABOUT the term. The Limit construct is a separate grammar
    term and is repetition; it is handed to setLimits, never to modify.


    7. OPERATOR ORDER -- LONGEST-FIRST IS A CONVENTION, NOT THE MECHANISM

    ⚠ Operators IS CREATED UNSORTED, AND IT IS THE EXCEPTION. getRegistry mints
    every registry with isSorted = 1, which is sortAscending; GroupControl then
    clears it for this one registry alone -- `opFields.isSorted = false`, beside
    `opFields.isOperator = true`. ⚠ THE DEFAULT WOULD INVERT THIS TABLE: ascending
    order puts every PREFIX above its long form, which is exactly the spelling
    this file is written to avoid. So the order below is kept longest-first BY
    HAND, on purpose, and survives only because nothing re-sorts it.

    Twenty-three registered operators have another registered operator as a strict
    prefix: the vertical-bar pair, the whole plus family, every compare. The table
    is written longest-first throughout, and as of 2026-09-16 it is, with no
    exceptions -- two were found and fixed that day, the vertical-bar pair and the
    add-member operator, which had been sitting below bare plus.

    ⚠ BUT THE ORDER IS NOT WHAT MAKES THE MATCH WORK, AND THE HEADER USED TO SAY IT
    WAS. It read that the table is loaded in descending order SO longest match is
    returned -- a causal claim, and it is false. Measured 2026-09-16, three ways,
    on the two rows that were out of order at the time:

      the two-character bar form answered its whole truth table correctly while
      registered BELOW its one-character sibling;

      the one-character bar and the caret both refused BY NAME in the same run, so
      the channel that would have reported a mis-read was live and reporting;

      the add-member operator answered 3 where a bare-plus read would have answered
      7, while registered eight rows BELOW bare plus.

    So the matcher returns the longest match irrespective of where a row sits.

    ⚠⚠ AND THE MECHANISM WAS READ, NOT INFERRED FROM THE BEHAVIOUR -- 2026-09-16,
    read-only, two functions. IT IS LONGEST-FIT, AND THE FIT IS DECIDED ONE LAYER
    ABOVE THE REGISTRY.

    testContainer (RuleStuff.twk) does a greedy CHARACTER-SET scan to build the
    longest run of characters that could belong to SOME entry -- an UPPER BOUND,
    never the answer, because a set has no notion of where an entry ends -- and
    then BACKS OFF ONE CHARACTER AT A TIME, calling get() on each candidate, until
    one is a real entry. It starts long and shortens, so the first success is the
    LONGEST candidate that exists.

    get() (GroupItem.twk) is a linear walk with an EXACT WHOLE-TAG COMPARE. It
    stops at its first hit, and since tags in a registry are unique that first hit
    is the ONLY hit. So where a row sits changes how many compares happen before
    it is found and NOTHING ELSE.

    ⚠ THEREFORE LONGEST-FIRST ORDER BUYS READABILITY, AND NOT CORRECTNESS OR
    EFFICIENCY. Not correctness: get() finds the unique tag wherever it sits. Not
    efficiency in any useful sense either -- the linear walk's cost depends on
    where the FREQUENTLY-MATCHED entries sit, which is orthogonal to how long they
    are. The convention is worth keeping because it makes a mis-registration
    obvious to a human reading the table. That is the whole of it.

    ⚠ AND THE UPPER-BOUND SCAN IS WHERE THE REAL HAZARD LIVES, not the ordering.
    Any container holding both a symbol and a WORD puts that word's letters into
    the character set, so the scan can build a run that is an entry of nothing:
    `--grup` scanned as `--g` and the whole match failed, until the back-off
    landed on `--`. Tony's finding and ruling, 2026-08-02, recorded at
    testContainer. It is a design flaw of set-based character grouping and not an
    edge case -- and no amount of row ordering touches it.

    ⚠ DO NOT PIN THE ORDER IN A FIXTURE: a row-order pin moves for reasons
    unrelated to correctness, which is rule H3. incant/pop/opPrefixT pins THE
    ANSWER instead, one discriminating row per pair, and it stayed byte-identical
    across the 2026-09-16 reorder -- which is how that reorder was shown to be
    inert rather than assumed to be.


    8. THE STRUCK BYTECODE ROW in fILEs

    Struck 2026-08-28 by Tony's ruling, from trim census phase one. Its file was
    deleted at cc2fd2d and nothing has included it since. A registered includable
    naming a file that is gone fails at exit zero with nothing saying why, which is
    bear-trap 28's fourth row.
*******************************************************************************/
